Z-- Language Specification (lang.doc)

Program Structure
-----------------
Every Z-- program starts with start and ends with end.

start
  // statements...
end

Comments
--------
// Single-line comment

/*
   Multi-line
   comment
*/

Variable Declaration & Data Types
----------------------------------
let number = 12       // integer
let name = Alice      // string
let score = 95.5      // float
let isActive = true   // boolean

Variables may also be declared without an initial value; they will be null until assigned:

let n
input(n)

Use let to declare variables. Type is inferred based on the assigned value.

Numeric Literals
----------------
Decimal:  42
Hexadecimal:  0x2A
Binary:  0b1010
Octal:  0o52

Operators
---------
Arithmetic
  +  Addition
  -  Subtraction
  *  Multiplication
  /  Division
  %  Modulo (remainder)

Unary
  ++x  Pre-increment
  x--  Post-decrement
  -x   Unary minus (negation)

Comparison
  ==  Equal to
  !=  Not equal to
  <   Less than
  <=  Less than or equal to
  >   Greater than
  >=  Greater than or equal to

Logical and Bitwise
  &&  Logical AND
  ||  Logical OR
  &   Bitwise AND
  |   Bitwise OR
  ^   Bitwise XOR


Sample Z-- Programs

1. Average Calculation
start
  let a = 10
  let b = 20
  let avg = 0.0
  avg = (a + b) / 2
  print(Average is: )
  print(avg)
end

2. Fibonacci Generator
start
  let n
  print(How many Fibonacci numbers do you want?)
  input(n)
  let a = 0
  let b = 1
  while n > 0 {
    print(a)
    let c = a + b
    a = b
    b = c
    n = n - 1
  }
end

3. Calculator with Nested if-else
start
  fun calculate {
    arg = (a, b, op)
    if op == 1 {
      return a + b
    } else {
      if op == 2 {
        return a - b
      } else {
        if op == 3 {
          return a * b
        } else {
          return 0
        }
      }
    }
  }

  let num1
  let num2
  let operation

  print(Enter first number: )
  input(num1)
  print(Enter second number: )
  input(num2)
  print(Choose operation (1=+, 2=-, 3=*, 4=/): )
  input(operation)

  let result = calculate(num1, num2, operation)
  print(Result: )
  print(result)
end

4. Grade Checker with else if
start
  let score = 82
  if score >= 90 {
    print(A)
  } else if score >= 75 {
    print(B)
  } else {
    print(C)
  }
end

5. Unary Operators
start
  let x = 3
  ++x
  x--
  let y = -x
  print(unary and update operators)
  print(y)
end

6. Numeric Literals (hex, binary, octal)
start
  let decimal = 42
  let hex = 0x10
  let binary = 0b1010
  let octal = 0o7
  let total = decimal + hex + binary + octal
  total = total - 5
  print(numeric literals and assignment)
  print(total)
end

7. Recursive Fibonacci
start
  fun fib {
    arg = (n)
    if n <= 1 {
      return n
    }
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)
  }

  print(fib 6)
  print(fib(6))
end

8. Array Operations
start
  let nums = [2, 4, 6, 8]
  let sum = nums[0] + nums[1] + nums[2] + nums[3]
  print(array sum)
  print(sum)

  let values = [1, 1, 1]
  values[1] = 5
  values[2] = values[1] + 4
  print(array assignment)
  print(values[0] + values[1] + values[2])
end

9. String Variables and Concatenation
start
  let message = Hello from a string variable
  print(message)

  let greeting = Hello,  + Z--
  print(greeting)
end

10. Input / Edge Cases
start
  let nameCode
  print(Enter a number:)
  input(nameCode)
  print(You entered:)
  print(nameCode)
end


Syntax and Snippets

Variable Declaration and Data Types

let number = 12       // integer
let name = Alice      // string
let score = 95.5      // float
let isActive = true   // boolean

Variables may also be declared without an initial value; they will be null until assigned:

let n
input(n)

Use let to declare variables. Type is inferred based on the assigned value.

Comments
--------
// Single-line comment

/*
   Multi-line
   comment
*/


Numeric Literals
----------------
Decimal:  42
Hexadecimal:  0x2A
Binary:  0b1010
Octal:  0o52


Control Flow
------------
if / else

if condition {
  // statements
} else {
  // statements
}

else if

if condition {
  // statements
} else if condition {
  // statements
} else {
  // statements
}

while Loop

while condition {
  // statements
}

for Loop

for init, condition, increment {
  // statements
}

Example:

for a = 2, a < 10, a++ {
  print(a)
}


Loop Control
------------
break Keyword
Used to exit loops prematurely.

Example:

start
  let n = 10
  while n > 0 {
    if n == 5 {
      break
    }
    print(n)
    n = n - 1
  }
end

Functions
---------
Defined using the fun keyword.

Syntax:

fun functionName {
  arg = (param1, param2, ...)
  // function body
  return expression
}

Example:

fun solve {
  arg = (a, b)
  return a + b
}

solve(5, 3);

Recursive Functions
-------------------
Functions may call themselves recursively:

fun fib {
  arg = (n)
  if n <= 1 {
    return n
  }
  return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)
}
print(fib(6));

Arrays
------
Arrays are declared using square-bracket notation. They are accessed and mutated by integer index.

Declaration:

let nums = [2, 4, 6, 8]

Read by index:

let first = nums[0]

Assignment by index:

values[1] = 5
values[2] = values[1] + 4

Note: indexing is 0-based and the backing C type is an integer array.

Strings
-------
String literals are enclosed in double quotes.

let message = Hello, Z--

Strings may be concatenated with the + operator:

let greeting = Hello,  + Z--
print(greeting)   // prints: Hello, Z--

A string stored in a variable can be printed directly:

let message = Hello from a string variable
print(message)

Operators
---------
Arithmetic
  +  -  *  /  %

Unary
  ++  Increment
  --  Decrement
  -   Unary minus

Comparison
  ==  !=  <  <=  >  >=

Logical and Bitwise
  &&  ||  &  |  ^  %


Notes
-----
Strings are enclosed in double quotes.
input(var) is used for user input.
Semicolons are not required.
Blocks are enclosed in { }.
The program begins with start and ends with end.
